  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The specimen is clear and auditable at the envelope boundary, but it is a weak answer to the unknown CAS object scenario. It keeps payload bytes opaque and avoids unsafe parsing, yet it does not define object-layer behavior for unknown CID codecs, and its own unknown-pCID rule is hard reject rather than opaque retention or forwarding.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit envelope, payload, and signature separation makes dispatch and parsing boundaries easy to audit.",
      "Deterministic CBOR plus mandatory signature slots provide a clear canonical-byte story.",
      "Fail-closed handling of unknown pCID avoids unsafe interpretation."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The spec does not say how a peer stores, advertises, or forwards a CAS object whose codec is unknown.",
      "Unknown pCID envelopes are rejected and must not be stored or forwarded as valid messages, which works against mixed-version relay behavior.",
      "Local diagnostics are allowed, but no durable rejection or relay record shape is defined."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Hard-reject behavior can partition long-lived mixed-version networks when new protocol or object types appear.",
      "Transport acceptance is tightly coupled to handler availability, weakening archive, bridge, and forward-only roles.",
      "If this pattern were copied into object-type binding, unknown objects would become non-relayable instead of safely opaque."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should payload protocols be required to support opaque storage and advertisement of unknown CID codecs?",
      "Can a peer retain unknown-envelope bytes as audit evidence without treating them as valid grid-envelope messages?",
      "What peer-local record should capture why an item was hard-rejected versus safely relayed opaquely?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
